        titleMy: "သရက်သီးက ဘယ်ကိုသွားခဲ့သလဲ",
        passageEn:
            "\"I just ate a slice of mango,\" said Nay Chi at lunch. \"What actually happens to it inside my body?\"\n\n"
            "\"Let me tell the whole story,\" said Moe Moe eagerly. \"First, your teeth bit and chewed the mango in your mouth, breaking it into smaller pieces and mixing it with saliva. That's the very start of digestion.\"\n\n"
            "\"Then, when you swallowed, the mango pieces entered your oesophagus -- a muscular tube that squeezes and pushes the food down, all the way from your mouth to your stomach.\"\n\n"
            "\"Once it reaches your stomach,\" Moe Moe continued, \"strong muscles churn the mango around and mix it with digestive juices. This breaks it down even further, turning it into a soft, mushy mixture.\"\n\n"
            "\"Next, that mixture moves into your small intestine -- a very long, coiled tube. This is where your body absorbs the nutrients from the mango, like its vitamins and sugars, straight into your bloodstream so your body can use them for energy.\"\n\n"
            "\"And finally,\" Moe Moe finished, \"whatever your body couldn't use or absorb travels on into your large intestine, and eventually leaves your body as waste.\"\n\n"
            "Tin looked amazed. \"So one little slice of mango goes on a whole journey -- mouth, oesophagus, stomach, small intestine, large intestine?\"\n\n"
            "\"Exactly,\" said Moe Moe. \"Every single thing we eat takes that same journey through our digestive system, with each organ along the way doing its own important job.\"",
