        passageEn:
            "Booth Two of the Science Fair covered three of the year's middle terms, and it was the loudest, brightest, and most magnetic booth of the day.\n\n"
            "Nay Chi ran the sound station, plucking a rubber band stretched over a small box. A clear note rang out. \"Watch this,\" she said, pressing her finger down on the band. The note cut off instantly. \"Sound only happens while something's vibrating. Stop the vibration, stop the sound.\"\n\n"
            "Next to her, Tin had built a small circuit with a battery, two wires, and a bulb. He connected everything -- the bulb glowed. Then he pulled one wire back, leaving a tiny gap. The bulb went dark immediately. \"Doesn't matter how small the gap is,\" he explained to a visitor. \"No complete loop, no current, no light.\"\n\n"
            "At the far end of the booth, Moe Moe held up a bar magnet and a small pile of paperclips. She lowered the magnet slowly toward the clips, and one by one, they leapt up to stick to it -- without her ever touching them. \"A push or a pull that works without touching is still a force,\" she said. \"Magnetism is just one kind.\"\n\n"
            "A visiting parent asked the trio, \"So which one of these is the most important -- sound, electricity, or magnetism?\" The three exchanged a glance.\n\n"
            "\"They're not really competing,\" said Tin. \"Sound needs vibration to happen, electricity needs a complete loop, and magnetism needs a magnetic material nearby -- three completely different rules, but each one is just as reliable as the others. Once you know the rule, you can predict exactly what'll happen every time.\"",
