        titleMy: "နှင်းဝေ၏ ကာချိပ်ကောင်ငယ် ဗူး",
        passageEn:
            "For a Nova School science project, Hnin Wai's teacher gives every student a small caterpillar in a jar with fresh leaves to eat. Hnin Wai watches hers every day, recording how much it grows. After about two weeks, her caterpillar stops eating, attaches itself to a twig, and forms a hard case around itself -- her teacher explains this is the pupa stage, and that this particular kind of pupa is called a chrysalis.\n\nInside the classroom, Hnin Wai's friend Thiri has been watching a grasshopper instead. Thiri points out that her grasshopper never disappeared into a pupa case at all -- it simply grew bigger each time it shed its old skin, looking like a tiny wingless copy of an adult grasshopper the whole time. Their teacher explains that Hnin Wai's caterpillar is going through complete metamorphosis with four very different-looking stages, while Thiri's grasshopper is going through incomplete metamorphosis with only three stages that all look somewhat alike.\n\nTwo weeks later, Hnin Wai arrives at school to find a beautiful butterfly has emerged from her chrysalis, slowly stretching its damp new wings in the morning sun. She thinks back to the tiny egg her teacher first showed the class, and realises she has watched the entire journey from egg, to larva, to pupa, and finally to adult -- while Thiri's grasshopper nymph has simply kept growing bigger and bigger into its own adult shape, without ever needing a resting pupa stage at all.",
        passageMy:
