            "For a Nova School science project, Hnin Wai decided to wake up early every day for a week and record the exact time the sun rose over Inle Lake near her grandmother's house.\n\nOn Monday, the sun rose at 5:52 a.m. On Tuesday, it rose at almost exactly the same time. Hnin Wai noticed the times barely changed from day to day, which made sense once Ms. Su Su explained that Earth completes one full rotation on its axis roughly every 24 hours -- so the sun \"rises\" for a given spot on Earth at almost the same time each day.\n\nHnin Wai also noticed something else: the sun always appeared first over the eastern hills, never the western ones. Ms. Su Su reminded her that Earth spins from west to east, which is exactly why the sun appears to rise in the east and set in the west, even though the sun itself is not actually moving around Earth at all.\n\nBy the end of the week, Hnin Wai had a full diary of sunrise times and a clear explanation for why they happened the way they did. \"It's not that the sun moves across the sky each morning,\" she told her class. \"It's that we're the ones spinning to face it.\"",
