        passageEn:
            "Every Saturday, Hnin Wai helped her father in his small bicycle repair shop near Nova School, mostly by handing him tools and sweeping the floor. This Saturday, her father decided it was time to teach her something new: how a bicycle's gears actually worked. He flipped a bicycle upside down and spun the pedals slowly by hand so Hnin Wai could watch the chain move between the toothed gears at the pedals and the toothed gear at the back wheel.\n\n\"Watch closely,\" he said, pointing at the small gear near the pedals and the much larger gear at the back wheel. \"When the small gear turns once, how many times do you think the big gear turns?\" Hnin Wai guessed correctly that the large back gear would turn less than one full time for every turn of the small front gear, because it had far more teeth to work through. Her father explained that this gear ratio meant more force reached the back wheel to push the bicycle up a steep hill, even though the wheel itself turned slower — exactly the trade-off between speed and force she had learned about at school.\n\nLater that afternoon, an elderly customer named U Tun Aung brought in an old mechanical clock to be repaired, since her father was known around the neighbourhood for fixing gears of all kinds. He opened the back panel and showed Hnin Wai a tiny cluster of interlocking gears, each one meshing with the next and turning in the opposite direction from its neighbour. \"The big gear moves the hour hand slowly,\" U Tun Aung explained, \"and this tiny one spins the second hand fast — different gear ratios for different jobs, just like your father's bicycles.\" Hnin Wai smiled, realising that the same idea of interlocking gears trading speed for force appeared everywhere, from a child's bicycle to the inside of an old clock.",
