        passageEn:
            "Htet Aung wanted to investigate catalase, an enzyme found in potato and liver cells that breaks down hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen gas. He cut identical small cubes of potato and dropped one into a test tube of hydrogen peroxide solution at room temperature, then measured how much oxygen foam collected in sixty seconds by reading a ruler taped to the side of the tube.\n\nNext, he repeated the exact same test, but this time heated the hydrogen peroxide solution to 80 degrees Celsius first. Instead of the vigorous fizzing he'd seen at room temperature, almost no foam formed at all -- the reaction had nearly stopped. Puzzled, he checked his notes from an earlier lesson on enzymes and realised what had happened: the high heat had denatured the catalase, permanently changing the shape of its active site so hydrogen peroxide molecules could no longer bind to it properly.\n\nTo confirm this wasn't just about temperature slowing all reactions down, Htet Aung ran a third test: fresh potato cubes in room-temperature hydrogen peroxide, but with lemon juice added to lower the pH to strongly acidic. Again, the foam production dropped sharply compared to the neutral-pH test. He concluded that catalase, like other enzymes, has an optimum temperature and pH range where its active site keeps the correct shape -- conditions too far above or below that range denature the enzyme and destroy its ability to bind substrate.\n\nIn his conclusion, Htet Aung emphasised that catalase itself was never used up in any of the three tests -- the same potato cube kept breaking down hydrogen peroxide continuously as long as its shape stayed intact, which is exactly what makes it a catalyst rather than a reactant.",
