        passageEn:
            "Wai Yan's physics class used a ripple tank -- a shallow glass tray of water with a motor-driven dipper that produces regular waves -- to measure the properties of water waves directly. A lamp above the tank cast shadows of the wave crests onto a screen below, letting Wai Yan see the wave pattern clearly frozen in place using a strobe light.\n\nFirst, Wai Yan measured wavelength by counting the distance between five consecutive bright bands (representing wave crests) on the screen and dividing by four to get the average distance between one crest and the next: 2.4 centimetres. Next, he counted how many times the dipper struck the water in ten seconds, then divided by ten to find the frequency: 6 hertz.\n\nUsing the wave speed equation, speed equals frequency multiplied by wavelength, Wai Yan calculated the water waves' speed: 6 Hz multiplied by 0.024 metres gave 0.144 metres per second. To check this, he also directly timed how long a single wave crest took to travel a marked 30-centimetre distance across the tank, and calculated speed as distance divided by time -- getting a very similar result, which confirmed his calculation from the wave equation.\n\nWai Yan then increased the motor's speed, making the dipper strike the water more often. As predicted, the frequency reading increased, and correspondingly, the gaps between the bright bands on the screen grew visibly smaller -- wavelength had decreased. Because the water's depth (and therefore wave speed) hadn't changed, this matched exactly what the wave equation predicted: if speed stays constant, a higher frequency must be paired with a shorter wavelength.",
