        passageEn:
            "Nanda Win's chemistry class built a simplified model of fractional distillation using a tall glass tube packed with glass beads, heated gently at the base with crude oil substitute (a mixture of several liquid hydrocarbons with different chain lengths) simmering below. As the mixture heated up, vapours rose through the tube, and thermometers taped at different heights recorded the temperature at each level.\n\nNanda Win watched as the vapour cooled while rising: near the base, where it was hottest, only the longest-chain, highest-boiling-point hydrocarbons remained as liquid, dripping back down. Further up the tube, where it was cooler, medium-chain hydrocarbons condensed and were collected through a side outlet. At the very top, where the temperature had dropped the most, only the shortest-chain hydrocarbons with the lowest boiling points remained as vapour long enough to escape through the top outlet and condense into a separate flask.\n\nComparing the three collected fractions afterward, Nanda Win noted clear differences: the top fraction was thin and ran like water, evaporated almost instantly when a drop was left on a watch glass, and caught fire immediately when a lit splint was brought near it. The middle fraction was noticeably thicker, evaporated more slowly, and needed the flame held closer and longer before it ignited. The bottom fraction, collected directly from the base, was thick and syrupy, barely evaporated at all even after several minutes, and would not ignite with the splint no matter how long it was held near.\n\nIn her write-up, Nanda Win connected the model directly to industrial crude oil refining: real fractionating columns work on exactly this principle at a much larger scale, exploiting the fact that shorter-chain hydrocarbons have lower boiling points and are more flammable and less viscous, while longer-chain hydrocarbons have higher boiling points and are less flammable and more viscous -- differences caused entirely by the strength of the intermolecular forces between longer versus shorter carbon chains.",
