            "Htoo Aung's teacher handed his lab group two unlabelled test tubes of colourless liquid hydrocarbon, telling them only that one was an alkane and one was an alkene, and asked them to figure out which was which using a chemical test rather than guessing. Htoo Aung remembered from class that bromine water, normally a distinctive orange colour, reacts specifically with the carbon-carbon double bond found in alkenes but not alkanes.\n\nHe added a few drops of orange bromine water to the first test tube and gently shook it. Almost immediately, the orange colour faded away to colourless. He repeated the exact same test with the second tube, using the same amount of bromine water and the same shaking technique -- this time, the orange colour stayed exactly as vivid as before, showing no change at all even after several minutes of waiting.\n\nHtoo Aung concluded that the first tube, where the colour disappeared, had to be the alkene: its double bond had reacted with the bromine, breaking the bromine-bromine bond and adding bromine atoms across the former double bond, converting the orange bromine compound into a colourless one. The second tube, where nothing changed, had to be the alkane -- with no double bond available, there was simply nothing for the bromine to react with.\n\nFor the write-up, Htoo Aung researched why this specific test mattered beyond the classroom: chemists and quality-control technicians in industry use the exact same bromine water test to check whether a batch of hydrocarbon fuel or feedstock contains unwanted alkene impurities, since the test gives a fast, visible, and reliable yes-or-no answer without needing expensive laboratory equipment.",
