      titleMy: "စာဖတ်ခြင်း — အေးချမ်း၏ ညှိုးနွမ်းနေသော ပန်းများ",
      kind: LessonKind.reading,
      xpReward: 10,
      readingPassage: ReadingPassageModel(
        titleEn: "Aye Chan's Wilting Flowers",
        titleMy: "အေးချမ်း၏ ညှိုးနွမ်းနေသော ပန်းများ",
        passageEn:
            "Aye Chan bought a bunch of fresh flowers for her mother, but noticed that by midday on a hot, sunny afternoon, the flowers had started to droop even though the vase was full of water. She wondered why. Her older brother, who was studying plant biology, explained that the flowers were transpiring rapidly in the heat — losing water vapour through stomata on their leaves faster than the cut stems could pull replacement water up through their xylem tissue. Normally, an intact plant's roots would keep absorbing water from the soil to match this loss, but a cut flower's stem has a much more limited water source. He suggested cutting the stems at an angle underwater to remove any air bubbles blocking the xylem, and moving the vase to a cooler spot away from direct sunlight to slow the transpiration rate. Aye Chan tried both, and by evening the flowers had perked back up, their cells refilled with water once the xylem could work efficiently again.",
        passageMy:
