        titleEn: "Thiri's Kitchen Observations",
        titleMy: "သီရိ၏ မီးဖိုချောင် လေ့လာတွေ့ရှိချက်များ",
        passageEn:
            "While helping her mother cook, Thiri noticed three different heat-related phenomena in a single afternoon. First, when she left a metal ladle resting in a pot of boiling soup, its handle grew uncomfortably hot within minutes, even though only the bottom touched the soup — she recognised this as conduction, heat travelling steadily through the solid metal. Second, watching the soup itself, she noticed bubbles and steam rising rapidly from the bottom of the pot, with the whole liquid appearing to circulate — this was convection, warm soup rising while cooler soup sank to take its place near the heat source. Third, when she stood near the hot stove without touching anything, she could still feel warmth radiating onto her face and arms — this was radiation, heat travelling as waves through the air without needing to touch anything at all. Thiri realised that a single kitchen moment could demonstrate all three methods of heat transfer working together: conduction through the ladle, convection within the soup, and radiation from the stove's surface.",
        passageMy:
