      readingPassage: ReadingPassageModel(
        titleEn: "The Great Umbrella Mistake",
        titleMy: "ထီးဆိုင်ရာ ကြီးမားသော အမှား",
        passageEn:
            "Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw are designing pretend umbrellas for a class project, testing different materials. Moe Moe makes hers out of paper first. As soon as their teacher sprinkles a little water on it, the paper turns soggy and starts to tear. \"That was a mistake,\" Moe Moe laughs. \"Paper soaks up water instead of blocking it.\"\n\nNay Chi tries a piece of thin fabric next. The water beads up and drips off, but a little seeps through the tiny gaps in the weave. \"Better,\" she says, \"but not quite waterproof enough.\"\n\nZaw Zaw wraps his umbrella frame in a sheet of plastic. When the teacher sprinkles water on it, every single drop rolls right off, leaving the inside completely dry. \"Plastic is waterproof,\" he announces proudly. \"That's why real umbrellas use plastic or specially coated waterproof fabric.\"\n\nTheir teacher gathers the class to review what happened. \"Choosing the right material for a job isn't just about what looks nice,\" she says. \"An umbrella needs to be waterproof, a window needs to be see-through, and a spoon needs to be strong. Every material has different properties, and a good design matches the material to the job.\" The three friends redesign their umbrellas using plastic, and this time, not a single drop gets through.",
        passageMy:
