        passageEn:
            "Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw visit the local market on a scavenger hunt to sort natural materials from man-made ones. Moe Moe finds a wooden basket used to carry vegetables. \"Wood is natural,\" she says, \"it came straight from a tree.\"\n\nNay Chi spots a cotton shirt hanging on a stall. \"Cotton grows on cotton plants,\" she says, \"so it's natural too, even though someone wove it into fabric.\" She writes it down on the natural side of her list.\n\nZaw Zaw picks up a plastic bag and a plastic bottle. \"These aren't natural at all,\" he says. \"People invented plastic in factories -- it doesn't grow anywhere or come from the ground like wood or cotton.\" He adds them to the man-made side of his list.\n\nThey also find a woolen scarf (natural, from sheep), a nylon rope (man-made, invented in a factory), and a stone mortar and pestle (natural, straight from the earth). By the end of the hunt, their list is full of both categories.\n\nTheir teacher reviews their findings. \"You've correctly sorted materials that come straight from nature, like wood, cotton, and wool, from materials invented and manufactured by people, like plastic and nylon,\" she says. \"Both kinds of materials are useful, but they come from very different places.\" The three friends compare their lists, surprised at how many man-made materials surround them every day.",
        passageMy:
