        passageEn:
            "Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw open an Inventor's Workshop to design new objects using everything they learned this term. Moe Moe invents a foldable rain bag: waterproof man-made plastic on the outside, which can be squashed completely flat for easy storage when not raining. \"I chose the right material for waterproofing, and I used a material that changes shape easily,\" she explains.\n\nNay Chi designs a natural-material lunch wrap using cotton fabric instead of plastic. \"Cotton is natural, comes from cotton plants, and can be washed and reused, unlike some man-made plastics,\" she says, folding the cloth around a sandwich.\n\nZaw Zaw builds a bendable reading lamp using a twisted wire frame that holds its shape once bent, paired with a rigid glass bulb cover that lets light shine through but keeps its shape. \"The wire is flexible for adjusting the angle, and the glass is rigid but transparent,\" he explains, demonstrating how the lamp bends into different positions.\n\nTheir teacher visits every workshop station, impressed. \"You've combined choosing the right material, changing the shape of materials, and understanding natural versus man-made materials into three brilliant inventions,\" she says. \"This is everything from Term 3, working together perfectly.\" The three young inventors proudly display their creations for the whole class to see.",
