        passageEn:
            "Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw create a Habitat Trail through the school garden to show off everything they learned this term, with four stops for visitors to explore. At the first stop, a model pond, Moe Moe explains why a fish needs water to survive, since it breathes through gills. \"This whole pond is the fish's habitat,\" she says, \"providing exactly what it needs.\"\n\nAt the second stop, Nay Chi has flipped over a log to reveal a tiny microhabitat. \"This isn't the fish's giant pond,\" she explains to visitors, \"it's a small, damp, dark spot within our garden -- home to woodlice and worms.\"\n\nAt the third stop, Zaw Zaw has drawn out a food chain on a big poster: cabbage -> caterpillar -> bird. \"The arrows mean 'is eaten by',\" he tells visitors. \"The cabbage is a producer that makes its own food, and the caterpillar and bird are consumers that eat their way up the chain.\"\n\nAt the final stop, all three friends work together to explain how everything connects: habitats provide what living things need, microhabitats are small habitats hidden within bigger ones, and food chains show who eats whom within those habitats. Their teacher walks the whole trail with pride. \"You've combined habitats, microhabitats, and food chains into one wonderful trail,\" she says. \"This is everything from Term 4, working together perfectly.\" The whole school walks the Habitat Trail and discovers something new about the living things around them.",
        passageMy:
