            "Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw go on a minibeast hunt around the school garden, each carrying a magnifying glass and a notebook. Moe Moe spots a line of ants marching across a path, each one carrying a tiny crumb. \"They're minibeasts,\" she says, \"and they work together as a whole colony.\"\n\nNay Chi finds a spider's web glistening with morning dew, stretched between two flower stems. \"A spider is a minibeast too,\" she notes, \"and it spun this whole web from silk to catch its food.\"\n\nZaw Zaw discovers a snail slowly gliding across a leaf, leaving a shiny trail behind it. \"This snail carries its shell wherever it goes,\" he says, watching it retreat into its shell when he gets too close.\n\nTogether, they also find a shiny beetle with a hard shell covering its wings, and a butterfly resting on a flower with its wings folded. Their teacher checks their notebooks. \"You've found five different minibeasts today,\" she says, \"ants, a spider, a snail, a beetle, and a butterfly. Even though they all look very different, they're all small creatures that live close to us, right here in our own garden.\" The three friends carefully release everything they found, excited to continue their minibeast hunt tomorrow.",
