        passageEn:
            "Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw learn that the minibeasts in their school garden aren't just interesting to look at -- they do important jobs too. Moe Moe watches earthworms tunneling through the vegetable patch. \"Our teacher said worms loosen the soil,\" she explains, \"so air and water can reach the plant roots more easily.\"\n\nNay Chi watches a bee land on a flower, collect something, then fly to another flower nearby. \"That's pollination,\" she says. \"The bee carries pollen from flower to flower, and that helps the plants make new seeds.\"\n\nZaw Zaw points out a line of ants carrying leaf pieces back to their nest. \"They all work together,\" he says. \"Some ants find food, some ants dig tunnels, and some ants protect the colony -- it's like they each have their own job.\"\n\nTheir teacher gathers the class to explain further. \"Without minibeasts like worms and bees, our garden would struggle,\" she says. \"The soil would become hard and poor, and fewer flowers would be pollinated to make new plants. These tiny creatures do enormous jobs for our whole garden.\" The three friends look at the minibeasts around them with new respect, realizing how much work these small creatures do every single day.",
