        passageEn:
            "Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw organize a Minibeast Safari through the school garden to show off everything they learned this term, with stations for visitors to explore. At Moe Moe's station, visitors identify minibeasts they find: an ant, a spider, and a snail, learning the word 'minibeast' for each small creature.\n\nAt Nay Chi's station, visitors count legs using magnifying glasses. \"This ant has 6 legs and 3 body parts, so it's an insect,\" she explains, \"but this spider has 8 legs, so it's not an insect at all.\" Visitors also examine a legless earthworm wriggling through the soil.\n\nAt Zaw Zaw's station, visitors learn about minibeast jobs. \"This worm loosens the soil for plant roots,\" he explains, \"and this bee carries pollen between flowers to help plants make new seeds. Even the tiniest creatures do important work.\"\n\nTheir teacher walks the whole safari, delighted. \"You've combined identifying minibeasts, counting their legs and body parts, and understanding their important jobs into one wonderful safari,\" she says. \"This is everything from Term 5, working together perfectly.\" The whole school walks the Minibeast Safari and gains a new appreciation for the tiny creatures living all around them.",
