        passageEn:
            "Zaw Zaw wakes up early, excited because it is two weeks until the school Sports Day. \"I want to run in the relay race,\" he tells his father at breakfast, \"but I need to get stronger first.\" His father smiles and suggests a simple plan: a little exercise every single day.\n\nOn Monday, Zaw Zaw goes for a short run around the school field with Moe Moe, feeling his legs and heart working harder than usual. On Tuesday, the three friends jump rope together at recess, counting how many jumps they can do without stopping. \"My coordination is getting better,\" Nay Chi says, smiling as she beats her old record.\n\nOn Wednesday, they practice cycling around the neighborhood after school, building strong leg muscles for the race ahead. Before each activity, their teacher reminds them to stretch first. \"Stretching keeps your muscles flexible,\" she explains, \"and helps stop you from getting hurt.\"\n\nBy the end of the two weeks, Zaw Zaw notices he can run further without getting tired. On Sports Day, he lines up for the relay race, remembering everything he practiced: running, jumping rope, cycling, and always stretching first. When the whistle blows, he sprints with all his strength, proud of the strong body he built one small exercise at a time.",
