        titleMy: "နေချည်၏ အအေးမိခြင်း",
        passageEn:
            "Nay Chi wakes up one morning with a scratchy throat and a runny nose. \"I think I caught a cold,\" she tells her mother, who feels her forehead gently. \"You must have picked up some germs somewhere,\" her mother says. \"Remember to cover your mouth when you cough, so you don't spread them to anyone else.\"\n\nAt school, Moe Moe notices Nay Chi coughing into her elbow instead of her hand. \"That's smart,\" Moe Moe says, \"it keeps the germs from spreading through the air.\" During lunch, Zaw Zaw offers Nay Chi a sip from his water bottle, but their teacher gently reminds them not to share cups when someone is unwell.\n\nThat evening, Nay Chi's mother makes her rest and gives her a warm cup of soup. \"Your body needs rest to fight off the germs,\" she explains. When Nay Chi asks for medicine to feel better faster, her mother is careful. \"Only a doctor or a trusted adult like me should decide what medicine you take,\" she says, giving Nay Chi a small, correct dose recommended by their family doctor.\n\nBy the next week, Nay Chi feels much better. She washes her hands extra carefully before returning to school, remembering everything she learned about staying well and not passing germs to her friends.",
