        passageEn:
            "Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw's teacher announces a new class project: building a weather station just outside their classroom window. \"First, we need a thermometer,\" she says, hanging one on a shaded post. \"This will tell us how hot or cold it is every morning.\"\n\nNext, Zaw Zaw helps place an empty container outside to catch rainwater. \"This is our rain gauge,\" he explains to a younger student. \"Tomorrow morning, we'll check how much rain fell overnight.\" Nay Chi ties a light ribbon to a stick and pushes it into the ground nearby. \"When the wind blows, the ribbon will show us which direction it's coming from,\" she says, watching it flutter.\n\nEvery morning for two weeks, the three friends take turns reading the thermometer, checking the rain gauge, and watching the ribbon, writing everything down on a big weather chart in their classroom. They draw a sun symbol for sunny days and a cloud with raindrops for rainy days.\n\nBy the end of two weeks, Moe Moe looks proudly at the full chart. \"Now we can see a pattern,\" she says. \"It was sunny most mornings, but it rained heavily three afternoons in a row.\" Their teacher smiles. \"That's exactly what scientists do,\" she says. \"They measure, record, and look for patterns to understand the weather.\"",
