            "Moe Moe's family needs to load a very heavy box of books onto the back of a truck. \"I can't lift this straight up, it's too heavy,\" her father says, trying to pick it up with his arms. Moe Moe remembers something from her science lesson. \"What if we use a ramp?\" she suggests.\n\nTogether, they find a long, sturdy wooden board and lean it from the ground up to the back of the truck, making a ramp. Moe Moe's father pushes the box up the slanted board instead of lifting it straight up. \"This is so much easier!\" he says, sliding the box up with far less effort than before.\n\nLater that day, Zaw Zaw's family needs to move a large rock blocking their garden path. Zaw Zaw's grandmother brings out a long, strong metal bar. \"We'll use this as a lever,\" she explains, placing a small rock underneath the bar to act as a fixed point. She pushes down on one end, and the rock lifts up on the other end, just like a seesaw.\n\nThat evening, Moe Moe and Zaw Zaw compare notes at school the next day. \"A ramp helped us move something up without lifting it straight,\" Moe Moe says. \"A lever helped us lift something heavy with just a bar and a fixed point,\" Zaw Zaw adds. \"Simple machines really do make work easier!\" they agree together.",
